 ## Overview
-Random Pixel Subsampling selects a random subset of pixel coordinates from a full image grid to reduce the computational cost of training volume rendering models, such as Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs), by evaluating the scene only at a fraction of the pixels per iteration [S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Two-stage NeRF Rendering](925-two-stage-nerf-rendering.md) by operating within a single training pass using stochastic gradient descent on a pixel subset rather than separating coarse and fine rendering stages, and from [Resolution Adaptation](3063-resolution-adaptation.md) by keeping the image resolution constant while varying the number of active pixels [G].
+Random Pixel Subsampling selects a random subset of pixel coordinates from a full image grid to reduce computational cost during training or rendering [S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of high memory and compute requirements when processing full-resolution images by allowing the model to learn from or render a sparse set of pixels [S2, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings, it relies on uniform random selection without spatial coherence or frequency awareness, making it a baseline for efficiency rather than quality [S2, S4, unofficial].
 
